A||||3||lowCG||Gearbox oil?||||5
Z||000000||lowCG||10-03-1999||02:14 PM||||What results have any of you had with different lube for the Muncie/Getrag 5sp?<P>I went to check the level in mine because it shifted a little rough,and it had 90W in it.<BR>Couldn't get myself to put 5W-30 in something with a ring and pinion/diff.,call me old fashioned I guess.Tried out some Redline 75-90W,one qt.of the gearbox kind,one of the diff/ltd slip kind.Works much better,even when cold,just curious what others have found. <BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Cooter||10-03-1999||03:27 PM||||I have Mobil 1 5w30 in mine and it still is a little tough to shift. I was hoping the synthetic oil would loosen it up a bit, but no luck. I guess the cables may need replacing- any suggetions???||||reg||14
Z||000002||Patrick||10-03-1999||03:37 PM||||<BR>lowCG, I know what you mean. In all my previous Chevs that had Muncie or Borg Warner manual transmissions, I've used 85W-140 oil. When I bought my Fiero 2 1/2 years ago, it had <B>ATF</B> in the 5-speed! I thought that was odd. It was then still a shock to find out that 5W-30 was recommended for the Isuzu 5-speed in my Fiero. I changed the fluid shortly after I bought the car, and I have to admit that I had to take a deep breath as I poured in the 5W-30. However, the tranny has been very happy since then.<P>I would recommend using the synthetic stuff.<P>It's possible that the rough shifting you reported is because the 90W is indeed much too thick.<P>By the way, my tranny is much quieter with the 5W-30 than it was with the ATF. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000003||theogre||10-03-1999||05:50 PM||||None of Fiero stock transmissions use "gear" oil. Fiero transmissions do not use helical cut ring and pinion sets.<P>As for stiff cables.... no easy fix their.... lubing them can be messy without a cable injector. even then you need to push thru enough lube to clean out the crud.||||reg||1
